ACCESS MODIFIERS :
These are the keywords that decides the visisbility of anything is java, ex class, methods, field/variables/members
1. public - Accessible from anywhere
2. private - Accessible within the class only(methods, fields/members)
             Top level class cannot be private it can either be public or default
             Nested class can be private and its accessible within that class only in which it is written
             Top-level class = a class that is not inside any other class.
             Nested class = a class that is inside another class

3. Default - Accessible within the same package only. And no keyword needed

4. Protected - Accessible within the package + Child class in any other package




Note : * Nested class can have all 4access modifiers and nested classes follow the same access rules as class members.
       * Java constructors can have 4 access levels.
            ~A private constructor means: you cannot create an object of that class from outside the class.
            ~ Then why use it?
              One common use is the Singleton pattern, where you want to make sure that only one object of a class can be created.
              Another simple use is when you have a class containing only static methods/utility methods and you don't want anyone to create its object.

       * Singleton Pattern = A design pattern that ensures only one object of a class is created and provides a way to access that object.
       * Singleton Class  = A Singleton Class is a class that allows only one object (instance) of that class to be created throughout the application.
            ~Singleton Class → The class that allows only one object.
            ~Singleton Pattern → The design pattern/approach used to achieve that.
       * Basically, in the Singleton Pattern, a private constructor + static instance + static access method are the core concepts.